def add(sub,b,title,why,facts,static,analysis,trap,mains,src='PRS',day=0,ap=False,p='P1'):
 J.append(dict(id=f'E17-2610-{len(J)+1:03}',date=f'2026-10-{day:02}'if day else '2026-10-31',date_precision='day'if day else 'month',subject=sub,block=b,title=title,why=why,facts=facts.split('|'),static_link=static,analysis=analysis.split('|'),trap=trap,mains=mains,priority=p,ap=ap,sources=['E17-'+k for k in src.split(',')],status='Historical October-indexed selected research. Proposals, approvals, future commencement and thematic revision distinguished; incomplete daily/monthly audit, not current-law or implementation certification.',checked='2026-11-07',evidence_access='Actual reading depth and primary/secondary limits are recorded in the source-access register and research/edition17/Research-Log.md. Static links, analysis, cautions and Mains prompts are original guidance.'))
add('Economy','01 · Monetary policy and financial stability','October MPC: a rate-hike consensus is not unanimity on stance','RBI’s 7 October resolution raises repo by 25 basis points and changes the stance to calibrated tightening. Its primary prose also corrects forecasts quoted differently in PRS.','Repo is 5.50%; SDF 5.25%; MSF and Bank Rate 5.75%. The rate decision is unanimous.|The stance changes to calibrated tightening, but two members preferred retaining neutral. A rate vote and a stance preference are different.|Primary prose projects FY2026–27 real GDP growth at 7.1% and CPI inflation at 5.2%, not PRS’s 6.7% and 5%. Conflicting chart figures are not silently combined with prose.','25 basis points equals 0.25 percentage points, not a 25% increase. Policy forecasts, observed inflation and realised annual growth differ.','Monetary policy can limit second-round effects of supply shocks through expectations and financial conditions; it cannot directly repair a monsoon or produce imported oil. A tightening stance does not guarantee a hike at every subsequent meeting.|For AP, trace transmission to farm/MSME borrowing, household purchasing power and state debt costs without assuming every loan reprices immediately. Retain the October forecast vintage rather than treating it as an achieved annual result.','The rate hike was unanimous; the primary text explicitly records two neutral-stance preferences.','How should monetary policy respond when supply shocks threaten broader inflation expectations?','MPC,PRS',7)
add('Economy','01 · Monetary policy and financial stability','Daily CRR maintenance: 99% of the requirement is not 99% of deposits','RBI’s 9 October notification changes the minimum daily maintenance requirement from the fortnight beginning 16 October. It is a reserve-management rule, not a 99% headline CRR.','Banks must maintain at least 99% of the required CRR on each day, up from 90%.|Average daily balances across the reporting fortnight must still meet the prescribed CRR requirement.|The notification invokes Section 42(1) of the RBI Act; it does not itself announce that the CRR ratio on liabilities becomes 99%.','A daily floor, fortnightly average and the underlying reserve ratio are three different quantities. Maintaining cash with RBI differs from holding eligible liquid securities.','A tighter daily floor reduces banks’ room to manage uneven liquidity within the fortnight. Assess payment flows, liquidity forecasting and access to markets rather than multiplying deposits by 99%.|Illustratively, if a bank’s required reserve is ₹100 crore, the daily floor becomes ₹99 crore; this example does not establish an actual bank-specific requirement. For AP banks, operational planning and liquidity costs may matter without changing deposit ownership.','99% refers to the calculated reserve requirement, not the share of deposits that banks can never lend.','Explain how reserve-maintenance design affects bank liquidity management.','CRR',9)
add('Economy','02 · Prices,ownership and economic institutions','Q2 inflation: observation month, release month and index base differ','PRS’s October review reports July–September inflation and September observations. The attempted MoSPI primary PDF returned 403; these figures remain secondary-attributed.','Q2 FY2026–27 averages are CPI 4.9%, food inflation 6.4% and WPI 10.4%, according to PRS.|September rates are CPI 5.5%, food 7.7% and WPI 11.4%; these are not October price observations.|PRS identifies CPI/food base 2024 and WPI base 2022–23. An index level, its year-on-year inflation rate and its quarterly average differ.','CPI measures consumer-basket prices; WPI covers a different price domain and basket. Food inflation is not the entire headline index.','Persistent food and energy shocks can affect expectations and vulnerable households differently. Compare consistent series and vintages rather than interpreting the gap between WPI and CPI as a mechanically fixed retail margin.|For AP, connect food supply, storage, transport and targeted support with national monetary policy. A quarterly average need not equal the final month’s reading; a blocked source must not be labelled fully primary-verified.','Published in October does not mean measured in October; 5.5% September CPI is not the Q2 average.','How should policymakers interpret divergent wholesale, consumer and food inflation?','PRS,CPI')
add('Economy','02 · Prices,ownership and economic institutions','IDBI strategic buyer: approved sale is not completed ownership transfer','The 14 October CCEA release approves a Fairfax subsidiary as strategic buyer following bidding. It gives more precise stake figures than the rounded PRS summary.','10955230 Canada Inc. is approved to acquire 60.72%, comprising 30.48% from the Union government and 30.24% from LIC.|Residual stakes would be 15% for government and 19% for LIC. Approved consideration is approximately ₹52,878.08 crore.|The release still requires buyer credentials and regulatory-compliance follow-through; a conditional approval is not independently verified closing or realised FDI.','Strategic disinvestment can transfer control as well as equity. Sale receipts, bank capital infusion, loan recovery and an investor’s promised future funding are different.','Evaluate valuation methodology, fit-and-proper standards, competition, governance and continuity for depositors. The release describes a ₹81/share winning bid versus a ₹75.99 fair-value benchmark, not proof that market price always measures intrinsic value.|For Mains, separate the fiscal receipt objective from banking efficiency and prudential supervision. Historical Prompt Corrective Action episodes do not establish that the bank remains under the same restrictions today.','60.72% is the approved transaction stake, not the government’s residual holding or verified completed transfer.','What safeguards make strategic bank disinvestment credible and accountable?','IDBI',14)
add('Polity & Governance','01 · Tax federalism and regulatory due process','57th GST Council: distinguish recommendations from enacted powers','The 8 October Council release proposes process and enforcement reforms. Some later rules are reported by PRS, but the complete instrument-specific commencement chain was not inspected.','The Council recommends omitting CGST Section 69 arrest powers, raising prosecution threshold from ₹1 crore to ₹5 crore and reducing general penalty ceiling from ₹25,000 to ₹10,000.|Small eligible e-commerce sellers could use an operator’s warehouse as principal place of business under proposed simplified registration conditions.|Refund proposals include a ten-day acknowledgement/deficiency period and risk-based provisional processing. Different ITC reforms have proposed November 2026 or April 2027 timing; not all changes share one start date.','Article 279A’s Council is an institutional forum for GST recommendations. Statutory amendment, delegated rules, portal capability and commencement are distinct steps.','Reduce arbitrary notices and working-capital blockage while preserving evidence-based action against fraud. Automation needs correction and hearing rights, not merely fewer human interactions.|For AP taxpayers, distinguish the amount of tax, ITC passed on and aggregate turnover when explaining eligibility. A press release recommending repeal cannot itself establish that a statutory arrest power has already disappeared.','The Council recommending removal of arrest powers is not the same as Parliament amending and commencing the law.','How can GST process reform improve both taxpayer trust and revenue integrity?','GST,PRS',8)
add('Economy','02 · Prices,ownership and economic institutions','Banking for Viksit Bharat: committee remit is broader than bank size','The 30 October government release constitutes a six-month high-level committee chaired by Suresh N. Patel. Constitution of a committee is not implementation of its future recommendations.','The review covers banking practices, prudential norms and regulatory/legal frameworks, including RRBs and cooperative banks.|Its remit includes long-term capital needs of banks, development finance institutions and NBFCs, and measures supporting private investment.|Priority-sector lending, the Lead Bank Scheme, AI/digital innovation, cyber safeguards and workforce capacity are specifically included.','RRBs, cooperative banks, NBFCs and development financial institutions have different structures and regulatory contexts. Financial inclusion is not measured by bank balance-sheet size alone.','Assess governance, credit allocation, resilience, consumer protection and competition alongside global scale. AI-enabled efficiency must preserve accountable lending and grievance correction.|For AP, link district credit coordination to underserved farm, tenant, women-led and small-enterprise needs. Do not assume the committee has already changed PSL targets or authorised a merger; those require separate verified instruments.','A six-month review mandate is not a new operative bank-merger or lending rule.','Which reforms can make banking globally competitive without weakening inclusion?','BANK',30)
add('Economy','03 · External stability and market discipline','Forex measures: FERR, underlying exposure and the oil window are distinct','RBI’s 10 October releases tighten specified rupee-linked derivative practices and open an oil-PSU dollar window from 12 October. Their thresholds should not be conflated.','The threshold for hedging contracted exposures without establishing the underlying exposure falls from US$100 million to US$5 million, subject to the stated aggregation rules.|Authorised dealers must maintain cash FERR equal to 20% of INR-equivalent notional for specified current-account foreign-currency purchase hedges exceeding US$2 million.|Cancelled contracts involving INR cannot be rebooked under the reviewed measure; maturity rollovers remain permitted. A separate window meets IOC/HPCL/BPCL daily dollar requirements.','FERR is not the country’s total forex reserves, an import tariff or a universal reserve on every foreign-currency transaction. A hedge and a speculative position differ.','Restrictions can curb duplicate or opportunistic booking but can increase legitimate hedging costs. Examine documentation, access and market liquidity rather than assuming a permanently fixed exchange rate.|For AP importers and exporters, match currency risk to genuine exposures. The oil-company facility is not an entitlement for every private importer or proof of free foreign exchange.','US$5 million concerns establishing exposure; US$2 million triggers the specified FERR rule. They are not one limit.','How can foreign-exchange regulation support orderly markets without discouraging genuine hedging?','FX,OIL',10)
add('Polity & Governance','02 · Digital markets and responsible communication','Securities advertisements: popularity cannot guarantee investment returns','SEBI’s October Gazette inserts a common advertisement framework. Opening primary definitions and commencement were read; detailed later clauses rely on PRS.','The amendment is dated 8 October, with 9 October publication header; it commences on the 180th day from publication, broadly April 2027.|PRS describes conditional celebrity brand/entity promotion, ratings with methodology and prohibitions on misleading claims, dark patterns and guaranteed-return assurances.|The framework covers multiple regulated securities-market entities; an advertisement’s disclosure does not establish that an investment is risk-free.','Regulator registration, an advertisement approval, product suitability and an investment guarantee are different. A verified performance/ranking framework cannot remove market risk.','Make sponsorship and risks visible without treating a celebrity’s endorsement as independent financial advice. Enforcement must address unauthorised promotions and prompt correction as well as registered advertisers.|For AP investors, connect digital literacy with complaints and verification of regulated intermediaries. Do not claim that every influencer recommendation has regulator approval or that the future code was already operative throughout October.','180-day deferred commencement is not immediate effect on notification; ratings are not guaranteed returns.','Can a common advertisement code reduce mis-selling across financial markets?','ADS,PRS',8)
add('Economy','04 · Enterprise finance and industrial capability','SME IPO consultation: eligibility, application size and trade lots differ','PRS reviews SEBI’s proposed changes to the SME listing framework, with comments until 4 November. The full consultation paper was not independently read.','The proposal shifts eligibility emphasis from paid-up capital to post-IPO market capitalisation, with ₹1,000 crore and ₹4,000 crore thresholds for specified routes.|Average operating profit of ₹3 crore over three years is proposed, with an alternative institutional-allotment route under stated conditions.|Minimum retail application is proposed to fall from ₹2 lakh to ₹1 lakh, and trading to become possible one share at a time rather than only in fixed lots.','Paid-up capital, market capitalisation, operating profit, net profit, application amount and trading lot are distinct measures. Smaller ticket size does not eliminate investment risk.','Wider access should be paired with disclosure, liquidity and market-abuse safeguards. An institutional route may improve price discovery but is not a guarantee of post-listing returns.|For AP SMEs, compare public-market readiness with governance and financing alternatives. Consultation thresholds are not certified current listing rules, and lower application size does not make every small company suitable for every retail saver.','A proposed ₹1 lakh application and a proposed one-share trade rule concern different stages.','How should SME capital-market reform balance access, growth finance and investor protection?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Economy','04 · Enterprise finance and industrial capability','SME Growth Fund: patient equity is not a subsidised loan','The 6 October Cabinet announcement commits ₹10,000 crore towards an AIF framework for growth-oriented SMEs, addressing a gap beyond early-stage support.','The fund is designed for direct patient growth-equity investments in viable, scalable small and medium enterprises.|Manufacturing-focused firms receive majority allocation; industrial clusters in Tier-II/Tier-III cities may also be considered.|The government commitment is not proof that the full amount has been invested or that every SME has an automatic entitlement.','Equity shares upside and downside and may affect ownership/governance. It differs from debt repayment, credit guarantee, working-capital discounting and a capital grant.','Independent selection, valuation, professional fund management, conflict controls and exit discipline are essential. Public capital should catalyse capability, not merely subsidise connected firms.|For AP clusters, connect financing with technology adoption, management, quality and export access. Track realised investments and firm productivity rather than assuming a national commitment creates a verified state allocation.','₹10,000 crore is a government commitment to the framework, not a universal loan waiver or grant to all MSMEs.','What governance safeguards should accompany publicly supported growth-equity funds?','SGF',6)
add('Economy','05 · Logistics and resilient supply chains','Container manufacturing: TEU capacity is not cargo already carried','The 28 October Cabinet release approves ₹10,000 crore through FY2039–40 for domestic container manufacturing. An unresolved component-total mismatch is documented rather than silently repaired.','The scheme supports new facilities and expansion, with operational expenditure assistance, 3% annual interest subvention on eligible term loans, and testing/certification support.|The target is approximately tenfold annual domestic manufacturing capacity, up to 7.5 lakh TEUs; TEU is a standard capacity-equivalence unit.|The release’s three component amounts sum to ₹9,900 crore against the ₹10,000 crore headline. The unexplained ₹100 crore gap is not assigned an invented use here.','A twenty-foot equivalent unit standardises container capacity; it is not a tonne of cargo or necessarily a count of physical containers. Manufacturing capacity differs from output and throughput.','Competitiveness depends on specialised steel, components, certification, finance and orders. Assistance should be tested against delivered quality, cost and durable market demand.|For AP ports and industry, consider supplier and testing opportunities without asserting a new local sanction. Projected jobs remain potential and scheme approval is not completed production.','7.5 lakh TEUs is a manufacturing-capacity target, not verified annual container output or port cargo tonnage.','How can container manufacturing strengthen India’s logistics resilience?','CMPS',28)
add('Economy','05 · Logistics and resilient supply chains','ITLA: integrate technical planning without conflating financial appraisal','The 6 October Cabinet release approves an Integrated Transport & Logistics Authority SPV to address fragmented planning across modes and ministries.','ITLA will prepare a national transport master plan with a horizon of ten years or more and assess alignment of shorter sectoral plans.|Technical appraisal covers central infrastructure projects costing ₹500 crore or more; monitoring is described for projects above ₹500 crore. The boundary wording differs.|Financial appraisal remains with existing mechanisms. The transport-data repository would combine sources such as e-way bills, FASTag, Vahan and GPS systems.','Technical feasibility, financial appraisal, administrative approval and realised transport benefit are distinct. Multimodal planning links modes rather than abolishing every sectoral institution.','A shared data model can reduce duplication, but access, quality, privacy and institutional authority matter. Post-implementation impact review should test freight time, reliability and environmental outcomes.|For AP, examine port–rail–road and urban connections without inventing a newly approved local corridor. A national SPV announcement does not mean every plan is already integrated or every data feed fully operational.','The primary technical-appraisal threshold includes exactly ₹500 crore; monitoring wording says above ₹500 crore.','What institutional changes can make multimodal infrastructure planning effective?','ITLA',6)
add('Polity & Governance','03 · Transport regulation and public interest','Bharatiya Vayuyan Niyam: consolidation and future commencement','PRS reviews notified aviation rules under the 2024 Act, replacing specified earlier rules from 1 January 2027. The complete Gazette was not independently read.','The reviewed framework consolidates licensing and operating provisions across earlier aviation instruments.|PRS describes tariff intervention moving to proportionate, time-bound central-government directions in extraordinary circumstances, while DGCA monitors tariffs.|Aircraft Management Service Providers require authorisation and operational responsibility under the specified non-scheduled framework; a dry lease supplies the aircraft without crew.','An Act and its delegated rules are different. An aviation operator, an aircraft owner, an AMSP and a lessor need not be the same entity.','Clear responsibility can improve safety, but regulatory consolidation must preserve oversight, enforcement and passenger interests. A change in one tariff power does not abolish competition or consumer law.|For AP airport development, separate infrastructure approvals from airline operating and safety obligations. Future commencement and the relevant service category must be checked before advising an operator about compliance.','1 January 2027 commencement is not immediate October operation; a dry lease is not a wet lease with crew.','How should aviation-rule consolidation balance flexibility and accountable safety oversight?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','01 · Clean mobility and energy choices','Flex-fuel testing: fuel labels describe composition, not universal compatibility','PRS reviews motor-vehicle rule amendments adding E100 and replacing a biodiesel testing reference with B100. Exact primary clauses were not independently inspected.','E100 refers to ethanol and B100 to biodiesel; the prefixes do not describe the same fuel.|Vehicle type approval and testing requirements are different from a nationwide instruction to sell only those fuels.|Fuel-system materials, engine calibration, cold-start behaviour and emissions determine whether a vehicle can use a blend reliably.','Ethanol is an alcohol; biodiesel generally consists of fatty-acid esters. Neither should be confused with every form of renewable diesel or compressed biogas.','Evaluate lifecycle emissions, feedstocks, water, food/feed uses and actual efficiency. Tailpipe carbon accounting alone cannot establish a fuel’s full environmental performance.|For AP agriculture and transport, connect feedstock economics with appropriate vehicle standards, not an assumption that every existing petrol or diesel vehicle accepts pure biofuel. Consult manufacturer and operative requirements for actual use.','E100 is not 100% biodiesel, and a test-rule change does not retrofit every vehicle.','What conditions are necessary for a sustainable and technically reliable biofuel transition?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Environment','01 · Pollution standards and climate accountability','Bharat Stage VII draft: in-use performance extends beyond new-vehicle tests','PRS reviews proposed emission standards with comments until 11 November. The primary draft and all vehicle-category exceptions were not independently read.','The draft combines pollutant limits with monitoring in use, fuel/electricity consumption, battery performance and ageing compliance.|An environmental vehicle passport is proposed to provide information on emissions, consumption and battery health.|PRS describes proposed manufacturing dates from April 2029 for specified passenger cars, April 2030 for light goods vehicles and April 2031 for larger vehicles. These are proposals, not verified commenced bans.','Vehicle certification, in-service conformity, greenhouse emissions and battery durability address related but different environmental dimensions.','Effective standards need test capacity, credible real-world data and affordable compliance pathways. Public information can support accountability but must be accurate, accessible and resistant to manipulation.|For AP cities, combine cleaner vehicles with public transport, inspection/maintenance and freight planning. A proposed standard does not mean all older vehicles must be scrapped immediately.','A consultation proposal with future manufacturing dates is not an October ban on every existing vehicle.','Why should vehicle-emission policy assess durability and in-use performance?','PRS')
add('Society & Welfare','01 · Social assistance and inclusive service delivery','NSAP continuation: new disability eligibility needs implementation follow-through','The 14 October Cabinet release continues the programme for 2026–31 and renames it Pradhan Mantri Samajik Sahayata Yojana. Guidelines are to issue and be implemented within three months.','The five-year national outlay is ₹53,428 crore. Existing old-age, widow and family-benefit schemes continue in their existing form under renamed arrangements.|Approved disability-pension assistance is ₹375 monthly for ages 0–79 and ₹625 for ages 80 and above.|The disability benchmark falls from 80% to 40%, and the minimum age criterion is removed to allow coverage from birth, subject to the programme’s other eligibility conditions.','Centrally supported assistance, state top-ups, disability certification and actual beneficiary receipt are distinct. A benchmark threshold does not alone establish every condition of entitlement.','Broader coverage needs identification, accessible enrolment, correction and timely fund flow. Low nominal assistance and care costs remain relevant even after a percentage increase.|For AP, do not replace the state’s pension rates with these central scheme figures or assume all approved changes were paid immediately in October. Track the operative guidelines and verified inclusion.','₹375/₹625 are the reviewed central scheme assistance rates, not a newly certified uniform total pension in every state.','How should social-assistance expansion balance coverage, adequacy and reliable delivery?','NSAP',14)
add('Polity & Governance','04 · Justice institutions and constitutional accountability','Tribunal service rules: appointments need independence and timely vacancies','PRS reviews October rules under the Tribunals Reforms Act 2026. The complete Act, Gazette and any subsequent court challenge were not independently inspected in this batch.','The reviewed rules specify qualifications for members of 15 tribunals and conditions for the National Tribunals Commission.|Serving judges/officers appointed to tribunals must resign or take voluntary retirement under the reviewed provisions; paid outside assignments are restricted.|Vacancy-filling should begin six months before vacancies arise. Complaint inquiries and member-selection arrangements are described in the rules.','A tribunal is a statutory adjudicatory institution, not automatically a constitutional court. Judicial independence, technical expertise and executive accountability must be reconciled.','Transparent selection, secure conditions, reasoned complaint procedures and timely appointments support credible adjudication. An institutional commission alone cannot guarantee independence or eliminate backlog.|For Mains, connect access to justice and separation of powers while avoiding invented holdings about an unread challenge. A notified qualification is not proof every tribunal has filled vacancies or reached adequate capacity.','A service-rule notification does not itself prove that all tribunal vacancies are filled or that judicial review is excluded.','What institutional safeguards are necessary for independent and effective tribunals?','PRS')
add('Polity & Governance','05 · Decentralisation and local planning','PM Viksit Gram Swaraj: digital convergence must strengthen Gram Sabhas','The 28 October Cabinet release approves a centrally sponsored scheme for 2026–31, linking panchayat capacity, planning, accounting and public communication.','The precise outlay is ₹7,395.55 crore, comprising central ₹4,869.48 crore and state ₹2,526.07 crore; rounded totals should not be mixed.|The design supports annual Gram Panchayat Development Plans through eGramSwaraj and online audit plans through AuditOnline.|No permanent posts are to be created under the scheme; need-based contractual support may be used. State annual action plans and approval mechanisms remain important.','A centrally sponsored scheme differs from a fully central-sector scheme. Portal integration, own-source revenue, Finance Commission grants and local democratic planning have distinct roles.','Improve usable data, functionary skills and interdepartmental responsibility so platforms support real deliberation rather than mechanically filled plans. Public disclosure and follow-up should be understandable to citizens.|For AP, connect GPDP priorities with water, sanitation, livelihoods and excluded habitations. Scheme approval does not transfer every departmental function to a panchayat or establish an AP-specific allocation.','The programme is not a permanent-government-job recruitment announcement or proof all plans and audits are complete.','How can digital convergence reinforce democratic decentralisation?','VGSY',28)
add('Economy','06 · Materials,procurement and industrial transition','Draft steel vision2047: capacity, production and emissions intensity differ','PRS reviews the draft National Steel Policy, with competitiveness, materials security and decarbonisation objectives. The full primary policy was not independently read.','The draft targets crude-steel capacity of 604 million tonnes annually and per-capita steel consumption of 302 kg by 2047.|It proposes stronger domestic scrap systems, coking-coal diversification, modernisation of secondary producers and value-added steel capability.|PRS describes an emissions-intensity ambition of 1.5 tonnes CO2 per tonne of crude steel by 2047. A lower intensity is not automatically lower total emissions.','Installed capacity, actual production, finished-steel consumption and exports measure different flows or capabilities. Coking coal differs from ordinary power-sector coal.','Assess the economics of scrap, clean power, hydrogen and process changes alongside demand and quality. Expansion without technology transition may lock in carbon-intensive assets and imported-input vulnerability.|For AP industry, connect plant modernisation, worker skills, logistics and environmental performance without inventing a new local sanction. A draft national target is not an achieved capacity or an immediate legal production quota.','604 million tonnes is proposed annual capacity by 2047, not current output or a confirmed AP production target.','Can steel-sector growth be reconciled with raw-material security and decarbonisation?','PRS')
add('Economy','06 · Materials,procurement and industrial transition','Domestic steel procurement: melt-and-pour is not merely final fabrication','PRS reviews amendments to domestic iron-and-steel procurement policy. Exact original amendment clauses and exceptions were not independently read.','Specified tubes, pipes and fittings must meet a melt-and-pour requirement rather than only a 50% domestic-value-addition test, according to PRS.|Melt-and-pour concerns steelmaking and the first solid form in India, not just cutting, coating or assembling imported steel.|Steelmaking central PSUs face domestic sourcing conditions for listed capital goods, with specified case-by-case exceptions where domestic specifications are unavailable.','Rules of origin, domestic value addition, quality certification and public procurement preference are related but different instruments.','Traceability can deepen capability but requires auditable upstream records and realistic supplier qualification. Domestic preference should not become a substitute for safety, timely supply or fair competition.|For AP manufacturers, check product scope, buyer category and operative exceptions. It is incorrect to infer that every private purchase or every import is banned, or that any locally finished imported product meets melt-and-pour.','Final fabrication in India does not necessarily satisfy a requirement that steel itself be melted and first poured in India.','How should public procurement support genuine industrial capability rather than superficial localisation?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Security','01 · Resilient infrastructure and system safety','Pipeline safety report: plan for geohazards and supply continuity','PRS reviews a PNGRB expert report on pipeline safety after exposure/rupture incidents. The full primary report and actual regulatory amendments were not independently read.','Recommendations include design-stage geohazard assessment for new trunk pipelines, with the stated CGD distinction.|Flood, seismic, buoyancy and vulnerable crossing risks require investigation; monitoring may use geotagging, drones, satellites and monsoon patrols.|Looped/interconnected CGD systems and alternate supplies can improve continuity, but the report is not proof all networks have already been upgraded.','A safety recommendation differs from an amended binding standard, an inspection finding and verified compliance. Network redundancy reduces some disruption risks, not all hazards.','Climate-informed design should consider changing rainfall and exposure, not only past averages. Emergency isolation, communication and restoration drills must accompany surveillance technology.|For AP pipelines and coastal/flood-prone areas, map crossings, community exposure and backup supply. Do not interpret “more monitoring” as permission to ignore defective materials or unaddressed geotechnical risks.','An expert report does not itself establish that every recommendation is already a binding regulation.','How can energy-pipeline regulation integrate climate resilience and public safety?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','02 · Hydrogen,safety and energy accounting','Hydrogen blending draft: 5% by volume is not 5% of delivered energy','PNGRB’s October draft explicitly says it does not itself authorise commercial supply. Scope and definitions were primary-read; later trial/billing provisions are supplemented by PRS.','The maximum commercial blend described is 5% hydrogen by volume, subject to lower applicable limits and specific approvals.|Entities must qualify their own network, equipment, meters, regulators and appliances; evidence from a pilot is not universal compatibility certification.|PRS describes trial operation, continuous concentration monitoring, automatic isolation, consumer notice and calorific-value billing adjustments under the proposed framework.','Volume share, mass share, heating value and lifecycle emissions are different. Hydrogen’s production pathway influences whether a blend actually reduces emissions.','Safety needs validated operating envelopes, leak detection, appliance compatibility and independent assurance. Equal gas volume need not deliver equal heat, so transparent billing and complaints are essential.|For AP, treat this as a potential pathway requiring site-specific statutory enablement, not an existing household mandate. A draft cannot justify unapproved fuel changes or a claim that every stove safely accepts any blend.','5% hydrogen by volume is neither a guaranteed 5% energy share nor a proven 5% emissions reduction.','What safeguards are essential before commercial hydrogen blending in household gas networks?','H2,PRS')
add('Polity & Governance','06 · Land compensation and infrastructure rights','Transmission RoW: verify voltage,scope and market-rate procedure','The 6 October Power Ministry guidelines address compensation for specified inter-state transmission lines. Complete operative provisions across the first two chunks were inspected, not all annexures.','The scope concerns tower-supported 66 kV-and-above ISTS lines, subject to stated market-versus-circle-rate objections and state-policy conditions; not every distribution wire.|Three district-selected valuers assess land. Extremes are normalised within ±20% of the median before averaging. Tower-base compensation is 200% of the determined market value for the defined area.|The operative text gives rural/other-urban/metropolitan corridor categories and a 30-day administrative appeal, conditional on allowing work pending adjudication. Administrative finality is not automatically exclusion of constitutional judicial review.','Right of way is distinct from acquiring full ownership. Circle rate, market valuation, tower footprint, corridor diminution and crop/tree damage serve different purposes.','Fair valuation and accessible contestation can reduce delay and conflict, but speed should not erase rights. Maintain transparent notices, landowner identification and upfront payment records.|For AP, first check applicable state policy and project category. Do not apply tower compensation to an entire landholding or assume the same corridor percentage covers every urban category.','These guidelines are not a universal rule for all voltage levels or proof that landowners lose every judicial remedy.','How can transmission expansion reconcile timely construction and fair land compensation?','ROW',6)
add('Economy','07 · Electricity networks and digital capability','DISCOM digitalisation: integrate reliable systems before adding advanced AI','PRS reviews CEA’s unified, non-binding distribution-sector framework. The complete primary framework was not independently inspected.','A readiness assessment should cover meter/consumer data, network maps, communications, SCADA, cybersecurity, procurement and finance.|The design favours progressive integration through a common network model rather than wholesale replacement of every existing system.|PRS describes foundational work in 2026–29, integration in 2029–32 and later scaling of advanced automation and analytics. These are proposed phases, not certified completed milestones.','Smart meters, GIS, SCADA and outage management perform different functions. A shared data model needs governance and interoperability, not simply a dashboard.','Poor source data can make sophisticated predictions confidently wrong. Establish asset IDs, communication reliability, security responsibilities and trained staff before automating consequential operations.|For AP DISCOMs, measure outage restoration, billing accuracy, complaints and financial outcomes. A non-binding framework is not a new automatic consumer disconnection rule or proof that every meter is interoperable and secure.','Non-binding guidance and a phased roadmap do not establish completed nationwide integration.','What foundations are necessary for trustworthy digital transformation of electricity distribution?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Security','02 · Defence acquisition and capability development','October DAC AoN: preserve the ₹2.12 lakh crore primary estimate','The 26 October Defence Ministry release gives an estimated ₹2,12,000 crore of in-principle capital proposals, more precise than PRS’s rounded ₹2.1 lakh crore.','Army proposals include mobility, self-propelled artillery, surveillance/strike systems, counter-UAS and long-range kamikaze drones.|Navy approval includes an Active Offboard Decoy System; Air Force proposals include helicopters, air defence, weapons and mobile X-band Doppler weather radar.|Approximately 82% of planned procurement is to come from Indian industry. This is not a claim of 82% domestic content in every subsystem.','Acceptance of Necessity precedes later procurement stages. Electronic decoys, interceptors, counter-drone systems and meteorological radar solve different operational problems.','Assess lifecycle maintenance, testing, interoperability, secure supply chains and delivery schedules. Large approvals cannot substitute for actual deployable capability or audited expenditure.|For AP suppliers, opportunities depend on qualification, production and contracting, not simply a national headline. Keep September’s distinct AoN batch separate to avoid treating two announcements as the same completed procurement.','AoN is not a signed contract, delivered equipment or an audited ₹2.12 lakh crore payment.','How should defence procurement be evaluated beyond approval values?','DAC',26)
add('Economy','04 · Enterprise finance and industrial capability','Mobile-phone incentives: incremental sales and genuine component capability','PRS reviews Target Segment1 guidelines under the Mobile Phone Manufacturing Scheme. The complete MeitY primary guidelines were not independently read.','The reviewed eligibility includes minimum global turnover of ₹10,000 crore in 2025–26 and specified annual incremental-sales conditions for existing brands.|Incentives are computed brand-wise with differentiated rate categories and annually rising baseline sales.|Additional component incentives require at least 25% of the total component quantity locally sourced and specified manufacturing processes; simple final assembly of semi-assembled products does not qualify for those additional incentives.','Global turnover, domestic eligible sales, component quantity and domestic value addition are different denominators. Incentive eligibility is not the same as realised claim payment.','Reward capability in components, skills, testing and supplier ecosystems rather than only assembly volume. Robust appraisal and claim verification protect public money and deter relabelling of imported inputs.|For AP electronics clusters, assess actual supplier development and workforce capability without claiming a new project-specific allocation. Border-country-linked ownership conditions and FDI approvals must be read in their exact legal scope.','25% component quantity is not automatically 25% of final-phone value or a universal local-content rule for all incentives.','How can manufacturing incentives encourage deeper domestic value chains?','PRS')
add('Polity & Governance','07 · Responsible AI and service accountability','TRAI AI draft: third-party tools do not remove operator responsibility','The 30 October draft reviewed by PRS invites comments until 27 November. The full draft was not independently read; requirements remain consultation proposals.','Telecom entities would classify systems by risk and conduct validation, conformity and impact assessment before deployment or material change.|An AI Governance and Safety Committee would review incidents, vulnerabilities, fairness and corrective action.|Operators would remain accountable for outcomes of procured or externally developed systems, with critical/high-severity reporting and compliance records.','AI governance involves the deployment context, data and operational permissions as well as the model. Accuracy alone is not fairness, robustness or explainability.','Preserve human review and remedies where automated fraud detection or network actions affect service access. Central incident records should improve learning without creating unnecessary exposure of subscriber data.|For AP users, test whether errors are corrected promptly and vulnerable subscribers retain essential connectivity. A committee or vendor certificate cannot itself establish safe deployment or transfer public responsibility away from the licence holder.','A draft does not create a currently verified binding rule; outsourcing an AI system does not outsource accountability under the proposal.','How should telecom AI governance combine innovation, reliability and consumer rights?','PRS',30)
add('Society & Welfare','02 · Health professions and evidence-based care','Allied-health boards: separate education,assessment and ethics functions','PRS reviews regulations defining state Autonomous Boards and a Central Assessment Committee. The complete primary regulations were not independently inspected.','The four state-board functions concern undergraduate education, postgraduate education, assessment/rating, and ethics/registration.|PRS describes boards with 10–20 members and professional representation, with specified experience/leadership requirements.|Where a functional State Council is absent, a Central Assessment Committee would perform designated board functions under National Commission supervision.','Professional education recognition, institutional assessment and an individual’s registration are different regulatory tasks. This follows, but does not duplicate, September’s registration note.','Clear mandates can reduce gaps and conflicts, but staffing, expertise and accessible procedures determine actual effectiveness. Professional participation should be combined with public-interest safeguards and reasoned decisions.|For AP colleges and employers, verify which body is competent and whether the state arrangements are functional. A national regulation does not prove every board has already been constituted or every institution is accredited.','Four board functions do not mean every state has four fully staffed operational boards as a demonstrated fact.','How can allied-health regulation improve quality while avoiding fragmented oversight?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Society & Welfare','03 · Education access and examination integrity','NTA committee report: security technology cannot replace accountable institutions','PRS reviews the education standing committee’s report on strengthening NTA. Recommendations are not independently verified implemented examination rules.','The committee identifies vacancies, vendor weaknesses, paper security, candidate difficulties and coaching-linked inequities.|Recommendations include parliamentary oversight, independent audit, specialised staff and a public record of disqualified service providers.|PRS describes a target of 1,000 secure computer-based testing centres by 2030, alongside exam-design, counselling and accessibility measures.','Computer-based testing changes delivery, not automatically question quality, equal access or institutional accountability. A recommended target differs from existing capacity.','Layered security should include procurement, secure content, contingency plans, audit trails and fair redress. Biometrics and AI can create new exclusion/error risks if treated as infallible.|For AP candidates, examine travel, language, disability accommodation and timely grievance resolution. Do not tell students that every exam has already shifted to a proposed format or that a parliamentary report guarantees an individual remedy.','A recommendation to expand CBT centres is not evidence that all 1,000 are operating or every paper leak is prevented.','What reforms can restore examination credibility while protecting equitable access?','PRS')
add('Economy','04 · Enterprise finance and industrial capability','Khelo India manufacturing: sports equipment is an industrial policy domain','The 28 October Cabinet release approves a sports-goods manufacturing scheme with ₹1,856.67 crore for 2026–2033–34. PRS rounds the amount to ₹1,857 crore.','Dedicated manufacturing zones would co-locate producers, suppliers, testing, design and prototyping facilities, converging with existing industrial infrastructure.|Anchor investment and turnover-linked support together have ₹1,130 crore; international certification support has ₹105 crore.|R&D, skills, existing cluster upgrades and export market development complement production incentives. Approval does not identify a verified newly sanctioned AP zone.','An industrial manufacturing scheme differs from athlete scholarships, medal awards and general sports participation programmes. International certification is product/standard-specific.','Quality, ergonomics, materials and reliable supply matter as much as price. Shared facilities can help smaller firms if access and governance are transparent.|For APPSC, connect manufacturing ecosystems and skills without turning a national programme into an automatic local grant. Evaluate certified output, innovation and durable employment rather than simply counting proposed zones or advertised investment.','₹1,856.67 crore is the approved scheme outlay, not an individual athlete’s assistance or already realised factory investment.','How can sports-goods industrial policy improve both export competitiveness and quality?','SPORT',28,p='P2')
add('Economy','08 · Minerals and resource governance','Critical-mineral auctions: bidder thresholds do not establish competition quality','PRS reviews amendments reducing the minimum number of technically qualified bidders for first-attempt auctions of critical-mineral blocks. The complete primary amendment was not read.','The reviewed minimum falls from three technically qualified bidders to two for the specified critical-mineral auctions.|Technical bids and initial price offers precede later bidding stages under the framework.|Fewer than the specified minimum still triggers annulment of that first attempt; exact later-attempt conditions require the operative rules.','Technical qualification, number of bidders, bid premium, mine development and actual mineral output are distinct stages. A licence does not guarantee an economically viable deposit.','Facilitating auctions may address thin markets, but transparent qualification, safeguards against collusion and realistic exploration data remain essential. Aggressive premiums can create later execution risk.|For AP mineral policy, separate national critical-mineral rules from unverified local block auctions. Assess environmental compliance, community rights and downstream capability alongside speed of award.','Two qualified bidders do not prove strong competitive outcomes or that a mine is already producing.','How can mineral auctions balance faster allocation and credible competition?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Polity & Governance','08 · Population data and urban transitions','Census enumerators: eligible recruitment pools and data protection differ','PRS reviews the Census Rules amendment specifying persons who may serve as enumerators. The primary Gazette was not independently read.','The described pool includes teachers across government, local-body, aided and unaided institutions.|Officials may come from public bodies, banks, companies, societies and other specified institutions; the state/UT may also select other suitable persons.|Enumerator eligibility does not alter every household question, establish survey completion or authorise public release of identifiable responses.','The Census Act framework, field-functionary appointments, training and aggregate publication are separate components of statistical administration.','A broader pool needs training, supervision, language skills and clear confidentiality obligations. Recruiting enough personnel is not equivalent to accurate enumeration or inclusive coverage.|For AP, plan multilingual household contact, migrant coverage and safe field operations without inventing district-specific appointments. Avoid treating a Census exercise as automatic proof of citizenship or individual entitlement to unrelated benefits.','Eligibility to serve as an enumerator is not unrestricted permission to disclose a household’s data.','What safeguards support accurate and trusted population enumeration?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Polity & Governance','08 · Population data and urban transitions','Census towns: urban statistics do not automatically create a municipality','PRS reviews the housing/urban committee’s recommendations on defining urban areas, including comparability and gender-inclusive methodology.','The reviewed Census-town criteria are population at least 5,000, density at least 400 persons/km² and at least 75% of male main workers in non-agricultural work.|A statutory town arises through state notification; Census classification alone does not necessarily establish an urban local body.|The committee recommends retaining criteria for Census2027 comparability while developing transitional/peri-urban planning and studying more gender-inclusive measures.','A Census town, statutory town, urban agglomeration and municipal corporation are not interchangeable categories. Male-main-worker criteria can miss women’s economic activity.','Administrative mismatch can leave rapidly urbanising settlements without adequate drainage, waste, transport or fiscal tools. Transition planning should preserve local representation and assess service demand.|For AP, evaluate peri-urban settlements around growth centres without assuming that census recognition automatically transfers every panchayat into municipal jurisdiction. A recommendation is not a completed boundary notification.','75% concerns male main workers under the reviewed criterion, not 75% of the entire population.','How should urban policy respond to settlements that are statistically urban but administratively rural?','PRS')
add('International Relations','01 · Bilateral cooperation and institutions','India–Switzerland: mobility arrangements are not unrestricted migration','PRS reviews the Swiss President’s October visit and cooperation outcomes. The full primary bilateral documents were not independently inspected in this batch.','The reviewed cooperation areas include sustainable infrastructure, transport, science and innovation.|Migration/mobility cooperation includes action against trafficking and support for professional exchanges.|The described young-professional exchange is 300–500 per country annually; it is not a general right for every applicant to work or settle abroad.','A bilateral professional-exchange arrangement differs from free movement, automatic qualifications recognition and an implemented trade concession. Switzerland is not an EU member.','Evaluate agency coordination, eligibility, recognition and measurable research/industry partnerships rather than counting agreements. Protect participants through transparent terms and anti-trafficking safeguards.|For AP students and enterprises, distinguish potential research and skill opportunities from a guaranteed visa or a new local investment sanction. Outcomes still depend on the actual instrument and implementation procedures.','A limited professional-exchange programme is not unrestricted immigration or automatic citizenship.','How can bilateral mobility cooperation support skills while protecting migrants?','PRS',p='P2')
add('International Relations','01 · Bilateral cooperation and institutions','India–Poland: a trade target needs an implementation pathway','PRS reviews the 21–23 October visit by Prime Minister Donald Tusk and proposed bilateral mechanisms. Primary outcomes bodies were not independently read.','The two countries announce an objective of doubling bilateral trade by 2030.|Defence-industry and counter-terrorism dialogues are described.|A visit, a dialogue mechanism, a signed contract and realised trade expansion represent different stages of cooperation.','Poland is an EU member. Bilateral facilitation and EU-level trade competences should not be treated as identical.','Build reliable business links, standards knowledge, logistics and sustained institutional contact. Defence cooperation also requires technology, procurement and export-control compliance.|For India, diversify partnerships without assuming that strategic cooperation means alignment on every international issue. For AP producers, identify market possibilities without inventing a confirmed Polish industrial project or an already achieved trade doubling.','A 2030 trade target is neither present trade volume nor proof that all barriers have been removed.','What makes bilateral economic partnerships deliver beyond summit declarations?','PRS',23,p='P2')
add('Agriculture','01 · Animal health and ethical research','Animal-disease rules: digital reporting must support rapid field response','PRS reviews rules replacing the 2010 framework under the 2009 animal-disease law. The complete primary notification was not independently read.','Bharat Pashudhan reporting covers specified outbreaks, vaccination and post-mortem records; PRS gives 12 hours after examination for the latter.|Veterinarians must maintain carcass-disposal records; a digital report is not evidence that safe disposal actually occurred.|The reviewed procedure gives notice/hearing and adjudication/appeal arrangements, with six-month decision periods for the specified proceedings.','Surveillance, diagnosis, outbreak notification, movement control, vaccination and disposal are distinct steps. One Health links animal, human and environmental risk.','Timely reporting needs field staff, laboratory access, farmer trust and compensation/support where relevant. A portal without response capability may record distress without controlling spread.|For AP dairy, poultry and livestock systems, promote accessible reporting and biosecurity without treating every animal illness as a confirmed notifiable outbreak. Administrative penalties require lawful evidence and procedure, not automatic blame on farmers.','The 12-hour post-mortem reporting rule is not a universal deadline for every administrative appeal.','How can animal-health surveillance combine timely reporting and effective local response?','PRS')
add('Society & Welfare','02 · Health professions and evidence-based care','Animal-experiment draft: operationalise replacement,reduction and refinement','PRS reviews proposed laboratory-animal rules, not a verified final replacement of all existing instruments. The full primary draft was not independently read.','Research, housing, breeding and trading establishments would need registration with CCSEA; experiments require the relevant prior permission.|Institutional Animal Ethics Committees would supervise defined activities; restrictions on protected wildlife/non-human primates have specified exceptions, not a simple universal ban.|The draft calls for scientifically satisfactory alternatives, fewer animals and reduced suffering; limited procurement exceptions require prior approval and veterinary quarantine.','Replacement, reduction and refinement are the3Rs. Ethical review does not by itself prove a scientific study is valid, and scientific value does not eliminate welfare duties.','Require justified design, competent care, endpoints, records and oversight of sourcing and disposal. Better study quality can reduce unnecessary animal use as well as improve research credibility.|For AP universities and laboratories, distinguish an institution’s registration from approval of a specific experiment. This note does not certify current permissions or authorise procurement from any unregistered source.','A draft with qualified exceptions is not an absolute nationwide ban on every animal experiment.','How can biomedical research uphold animal welfare and scientific quality together?','PRS',p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','03 · Nobel science and research methods','Physics Nobel2026: IceCube opens a neutrino window on the universe','The official 6 October release awards Francis Halzen for contributions to IceCube and high-energy astrophysical neutrino discovery. The award date is not the detector’s construction date.','IceCube uses light sensors in approximately a cubic kilometre of South Pole ice; the detector was completed in2011.|Rare neutrino interactions produce secondary charged particles whose light can be detected and reconstructed.|Neutrinos are electrically neutral and weakly interacting, making them useful messengers from energetic cosmic environments.','Neutrino detection differs from ordinary optical imaging. The observed light comes from interaction products; neutrality does not mean a particle has no mass or never interacts.','Large detectors, low background, calibration and international collaboration turn rare events into credible evidence. Scientific interpretation requires statistical confidence and careful reconstruction rather than a single unexplained flash.|For Prelims, distinguish IceCube’s Antarctic location from India’s proposed neutrino facility. The2026award recognises a long research programme, not a new2026completion of the instrument.','IceCube is at the South Pole, not an optical telescope in space or an Indian commissioned observatory.','Why do neutrinos complement electromagnetic observations in multi-messenger astronomy?','NPHY',6,p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','03 · Nobel science and research methods','Chemistry Nobel2026: amplify asymmetry without oversimplifying chirality','The official 7 October announcement honours Henri B. Kagan and Kenso Soai for non-linear effects and autocatalysis in asymmetric organic synthesis.','Enantiomers are non-superimposable mirror-image forms of a chiral molecule.|Non-linear effects and asymmetric autocatalysis can amplify a small initial asymmetry, helping explain routes towards highly enriched products.|Biological chemistry frequently favours particular handedness, but not every amino acid is chiral: glycine is an important exception.','A catalyst changes reaction pathways without simply being consumed as a normal reactant; autocatalysis involves a product promoting its own formation. Chirality differs from a molecule merely being drawn in a different orientation.','Selective synthesis matters for pharmaceuticals because mirror forms can interact differently with biological targets. That does not mean one enantiomer is always beneficial and the other always poisonous.|The press-release popularisation is not a complete proof of life’s origin. For exam answers, connect molecular asymmetry with research methods while preserving the distinction between laboratory mechanisms and historical origin hypotheses.','Glycine is achiral; it is incorrect to say every amino acid necessarily has a pair of chiral enantiomers.','Explain why control of molecular handedness matters in chemistry and medicine.','NCHEM',7,p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','03 · Nobel science and research methods','Medicine Nobel2026: optogenetics links targeted light control to neural function','The official 5 October announcement honours Karl Deisseroth, Peter Hegemann and Georg Nagel for light-gated ion channels and optogenetics.','Channelrhodopsin discovery in algae supplied a light-sensitive ion-channel tool.|Introducing appropriate genetic instructions into selected cells can make their activity respond to light, enabling controlled studies of neural circuits.|Optogenetics can test causal roles of selected cells; it is not simply recording brain images or shining ordinary light at any unmodified brain.','Ion channels, genes, electrical signalling and optical stimulation contribute different parts of the method. Experimental capability is not identical to an approved clinical treatment.','Precise targeting and timing can improve understanding of behaviour and disease, but delivery, safety and ethical constraints remain. Human therapeutic development requires evidence beyond animal demonstrations.|For AP medical/research contexts, connect foundational biology to rigorous translation without promising a universal cure for neurological or visual conditions. Award recognition does not itself license a new therapy or erase informed-consent obligations.','Optogenetics requires appropriate light-responsive biological machinery; ordinary external illumination is not the same intervention.','How do targeted experimental tools improve causal understanding in neuroscience?','NMED',5,p='P2')
add('Economy','01 · Monetary policy and financial stability','Economic Sciences2026: expectations connect households,firms and stabilisation','The official 12 October announcement honours Robert E. Hall and Michael Woodford for microeconomic foundations of macroeconomic stabilisation.','The recognised work connects individual household and firm decisions with aggregate economic fluctuations.|Forward-looking expectations help explain how policy communication affects present consumption, investment and pricing.|Fiscal and monetary stabilisation are distinct instruments; a model provides conditional analytical guidance, not an infallible forecast.','Microfoundations concern behaviour and constraints beneath aggregate relationships. Sticky prices and expectations can affect transmission, but no single assumption describes every economy perfectly.','Credible communication must be backed by institutions and feasible action. Stabilisation may face difficult trade-offs during supply shocks, when weakening demand cannot directly create missing output.|Link to October RBI policy without claiming the prize proves every decision optimal. In Indian/AP answers, include distribution, informality and data limitations when applying general models. This is a new award for accumulated research, not a2026invention of all expectations-based economics.','The Economic Sciences prize is the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Memory of Alfred Nobel; it is not a newly established Indian monetary-policy rule.','How do expectations and microeconomic behaviour influence macroeconomic stabilisation?','NECO',12,p='P2')
add('International Relations','02 · Global justice and public institutions','Peace Nobel2026: Navi Pillay and the institutions of international justice','The Norwegian Nobel Committee’s 9 October announcement recognises Navanethem “Navi” Pillay for advancing peace and international law.','The release highlights her South African legal background and service in international human-rights and criminal-justice institutions.|It connects accountability for atrocities with the development of legal norms and protections for victims.|An award recognises work; it does not give its recipient new jurisdiction or decide the merits of every ongoing case.','The International Court of Justice deals principally with disputes between states; the International Criminal Court addresses individual criminal responsibility within its jurisdiction. They are not one court.','International justice depends on evidence, jurisdiction, cooperation and independence; institutional power is uneven and enforcement can be contested. Recognition should not replace examination of particular judgments or legal standards.|For Mains, assess the relationship between peace, accountability and diplomacy without asserting that any award alone resolves conflict. Distinguish a commission of inquiry, a criminal tribunal and a state-dispute court.','The Norwegian Nobel Committee awards the Peace Prize; it is not the body awarding the science prizes.','Can international legal accountability contribute to durable peace?','NPEACE',9,p='P2')
add('Culture & History','01 · Literature and public memory','Literature Nobel2026: Anne Carson and dialogue with classical traditions','The Swedish Academy’s 8 October release awards the Literature Prize to Canadian author Anne Carson. The short official announcement, not a complete bibliography, was read.','The citation recognises an inventive body of work engaging with classical tradition and new contemporary literary forms.|A literary oeuvre means a body of work, not necessarily one winning book.|The Swedish Academy awards the Literature Prize; it differs from the Norwegian Nobel Committee and the Royal Swedish Academy of Sciences.','Genre experimentation, translation and classical reception can connect ancient texts with new literary expression. A prize citation is an evaluative statement, not a historical primary source for every theme in the author’s work.','For factual recall, pair author, country, prize category and awarding institution accurately. Do not invent a single “prize-winning novel” when the announcement honours the body of work.|For wider study, compare how translation and reinterpretation keep heritage active without reducing literature to a ranking. This note does not claim all of Carson’s books were independently read or reviewed in this batch.','The Literature award is to Anne Carson’s body of work, not a named novel invented from the citation.','How can engagement with classical traditions generate contemporary literary innovation?','NLIT',8,p='P3')
add('Environment','02 · Climate pathways and risk finance','Emissions Gap2026: a return pathway is conditional,not a guarantee','UNEP’s 21 October explainer summarises The Point of Return. The substantive explainer was read, not the complete technical report.','The explainer distinguishes projected warming under current policies at2.8°C, full pledges at2.6°C and additional net-zero commitments at2.1°C.|Its lowest-overshoot pathway needs stringent emissions reductions and substantial removals; it gives a50%chance of returning to1.5°Cby2100under the stated conditions.|Around320gigatonnes of CO2 removal by century-end feature in that pathway, with major feasibility and permanence uncertainties.','A policy projection, conditional pledge scenario and probabilistic pathway are different. Annual temperature variation is not identical to long-term global warming.','Reduce emissions rapidly rather than treating future removals as a substitute. Every additional period of overshoot increases risks that may be difficult or impossible to reverse.|For AP, connect coastal assets, heat, agriculture and water to both mitigation and adaptation. Do not interpret a conditional50%probability as certain cooling or claim that returning below a threshold repairs every ecosystem loss.','320gigatonnes is CO2 removal in a conditional pathway,not a verified quantity already removed or an annual target.','Why must climate policy limit both the magnitude and duration of warming overshoot?','UNEP',21)
add('Environment','03 · Biodiversity indicators and coastal ecosystems','Living Planet reporting: average population decline is not species extinction','Selected October magazine passages discuss Living Planet Report2026. The primary dataset/report was not independently read, so exact numerical headlines are not adopted as verified facts.','The Living Planet Index tracks relative abundance trends in monitored vertebrate populations.|A percentage decline in an aggregate population index is not the percentage of all animals killed or all species extinct.|Baseline year, geographic coverage, monitoring selection and aggregation affect interpretation; an apparently smaller recent decline need not mean an ecosystem was historically intact.','Population abundance,species richness,extinction risk and ecosystem condition measure different dimensions of biodiversity. A geometric aggregation behaves differently from adding all animals together.','Use indicators to guide habitat restoration,water protection and sustainable production while retaining methods and uncertainty. Positive recovery examples do not invalidate serious overall pressures, and global averages do not describe every species.|For AP,link local monitoring of wetlands,coasts and forest habitats to management decisions rather than assigning a global index directly to state biodiversity. Exact regional figures require the original report and its reference period.','A73%index decline,if quoted with its proper source and period,would not mean73%of species have become extinct.','How should biodiversity indicators be used without misleading the public?','MAG',p='P2')
add('Environment','02 · Climate pathways and risk finance','Parametric climate insurance: fast payouts can leave basis risk','Selected magazine passages discuss risk pools and protection gaps. Specific newly operational national pools,premium subsidies and coverage totals were not independently verified.','Parametric insurance pays according to a predefined observable trigger rather than a conventional individual-loss assessment.|A trigger can speed payment,but a household may suffer loss without the index reaching the threshold,or receive payment without matching loss.|Risk pooling and reinsurance spread financial exposure;they do not prevent the underlying hazard or automatically make all coverage affordable.','Basis risk is the mismatch between trigger outcome and actual loss. Hazard,exposure,vulnerability,premium and insured limit are different concepts.','Use credible data,transparent triggers,appropriate geography and accessible complaints. Public subsidy decisions should be transparent and linked to inclusion,not hidden guarantees for risky development.|For AP coastal and agricultural livelihoods,combine financial protection with warning,safe infrastructure and resilient siting. A magazine policy recommendation is not proof that every household is enrolled or that relief assistance and insurance are interchangeable.','Parametric insurance does not guarantee payment for every actual loss;basis risk remains central.','How can climate-risk finance support vulnerable communities without replacing prevention?','MAG')
add('Environment','03 · Biodiversity indicators and coastal ecosystems','National Coastal Mission2.0: integrate ecosystems and livelihoods','The October compilation revisits mission guidelines and integrated coastal management. This is selected thematic consolidation;the complete primary guidelines and exact rollout were not independently read.','Integrated coastal-zone planning connects land use,water,ecosystems,infrastructure and community livelihoods.|Mangroves,brackish wetlands and coral systems require site-appropriate conservation rather than one uniform intervention.|A mission framework and spatial plan do not automatically replace applicable coastal regulation,local rights or project-specific environmental permissions.','Blue economy is broader than port expansion. Ecosystem restoration,coastal zoning and disaster-risk management are complementary but distinct governance tasks.','Coordinate agencies and communities to avoid shifting erosion,pollution or livelihood losses from one location to another. Restoration quality and survival matter more than only planted area or allocated funds.|For AP,connect fisheries,aquaculture,ports,tourism and cyclone resilience with cumulative-impact assessment. Do not present an unverified guideline launch as proof every state project has received a new sanction or clearance.','A national mission is not blanket permission to build in every coastal zone or clear mangroves.','How should integrated coastal management reconcile economic development and ecosystem resilience?','MAG',p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','04 · Storage,quantum systems and applied research','Pumped storage: shifting electricity requires energy input and suitable sites','The October magazine reviews pumped-storage projects and grid integration. Exact national project totals,potential estimates and primary approval exceptions were not independently checked.','Surplus electricity pumps water uphill;later release through turbines returns electricity when needed.|Pumped storage is an energy-storage system with conversion losses,not a net source of free energy.|On-stream,open-loop and closed-loop settings have different hydrological/ecological relationships;off-stream does not automatically mean zero environmental impact.','Power rating inMWorGWdiffers from energy storage inMWhorGWh. Duration depends on usable energy and discharge power.','Assess geology,water balance,evaporation,land,grid connections,cycling and commercial arrangements. Installed capacity is not equal to dependable peak service without water and operational readiness.|For AP,compare storage options against actual demand and renewable profiles rather than assuming every reservoir can be cheaply converted. A national theoretical potential estimate is not a list of already approved or commissioned state projects.','Pumping and generation cannot together create unlimited net energy;round-trip efficiency is below100%.','What determines whether a pumped-storage project provides useful and sustainable grid services?','MAG',p='P2')
add('Science & Technology','04 · Storage,quantum systems and applied research','Free-space QKD: secure keys are not an entire unbreakable network','A selected magazine passage reports a5.56kmfree-space demonstration linking BISAG-N and IIT Gandhinagar with QNuLabs. The primary test report was not independently read;performance/first-in-India claims remain unverified.','QKD uses quantum signals to establish keys;ordinary user data can still travel through conventional encrypted communication.|Free-space channels need line of sight,alignment and control of atmospheric/background effects.|QKD and post-quantum cryptography are different approaches and may be combined;neither removes endpoint,software or implementation risks.','Eavesdropping detection in an ideal protocol is not the same as immunity to every real-world attack. A channel demonstration differs from a production network or satellite service.','Evaluate authenticated classical communication,key management,devices,availability and independent security testing. Weather and outages can affect usability even when laboratory security properties are strong.|For AP strategic/digital infrastructure,link quantum-readiness with practical cybersecurity hygiene. Do not convert a short demonstration into a claim of a fully deployed national quantum internet or assume every security layer has been audited.','QKD distributes keys;it does not automatically carry all data quantum-mechanically or make compromised endpoints safe.','How should quantum communication be evaluated alongside conventional cybersecurity?','MAG',p='P2')
add('Polity & Governance','04 · Justice institutions and constitutional accountability','RTI at21: proactive disclosure matters as much as appeal machinery','The October magazine’s opening infographic reviews21years since the Act became operational on12October2005. The cited civil-society report and every latest amendment/commencement instrument were not independently inspected.','Section4proactive disclosure can reduce the need for individual information requests.|PIOs,first appellate authorities and information commissions perform different stages in the access/redress framework.|The reviewed infographic highlights vacancies,delays,procedural returns and privacy tensions,but exact pendency/death statistics are withheld pending primary checks.','The right to know connects with Article19(1)(a),while privacy and legitimate statutory exemptions also matter. Access to existing records is not an unlimited demand to create new explanations.','Usable,machine-readable disclosure,record management,timely appointments and reasoned orders can improve access. Digitisation should assist applicants,not create a new barrier for people without connectivity.|For AP,link RTI with pensions,local works and service accountability without certifying the latest operative wording of every personal-information clause from a magazine alone. Requests,appeals and complaints are distinct remedies.','An anniversary review is not proof that every information commission is functional or every requested record is disclosable.','How can RTI implementation strengthen everyday administrative accountability?','MAG',12)
add('Culture & History','02 · Institutions and constitutional evolution','Public Service Commission centenary: distinguish1926from constitutional UPSC','The October compilation marks a century since the first Public Service Commission was established on1October1926. This historical sequence is not a new constitutional institution created in2026.','The first commission began under the colonial framework in1926.|The Federal Public Service Commission followed under the1935Act framework;constitutional UPSC belongs to the republic from1950.|Modern constitutional provisions for Union and State Public Service Commissions are in Articles315–323.','A colonial advisory institution,the Federal Commission and present constitutional UPSC are historically connected but legally distinct. A recruitment commission is not the same as a training academy or appointing government.','Examine merit,independence,accessibility,representation and examination integrity alongside institutional longevity. Technology can improve administration only if accompanied by privacy and fair grievance procedures.|For APPSC,compare constitutional safeguards and responsibilities while preserving the distinction between Union and State jurisdictions. The centenary does not imply every modern constitutional power existed unchanged in1926.','1926marks the first Public Service Commission,not the commencement of the1950Constitution or a century of identical UPSC powers.','How do constitutional safeguards support impartial public-service recruitment?','MAG',1,p='P2')
add('Agriculture','02 · Forest rights and livelihood convergence','FRA cultivation support: improve livelihoods without encouraging fresh clearance','Selected magazine passages review the agricultural potential of land held under recognised forest rights. Exact title totals and all scheme eligibility rules were not independently checked.','Individual forest rights recognise eligible pre-existing occupation/cultivation under the statutory framework;they are not a new licence to clear additional forest.|Recognised cultivated area and the statutory maximum must be distinguished;an eligible person does not automatically receive a full4hectares.|Agroecology,millets,pulses,horticulture,non-timber products and suitable agroforestry can support livelihoods,subject to local rights and ecology.','Individual cultivation rights,community rights and community forest-resource rights are distinct. Recognition of rights should not be collapsed into unrestricted commercial transferability.','Converge extension,water,credit,market access and nutrition while protecting consent,biodiversity and women’s participation. A title alone does not ensure viable income if transport and service access remain weak.|For AP tribal areas,verify cultivation evidence and local decisions without treating every applicant as entitled to a uniform parcel or assuming programme convergence overrides conservation duties. The note is thematic application,not a new October allotment order.','FRA does not authorise fresh forest clearance or automatically allot4hectares to every household.','How can rights recognition and livelihood support be combined in forest landscapes?','MAG')
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Higher education and student support','AP foreign-university policy: approval is not an operational campus','Reports of the7OctoberCabinet describe the Andhra Pradesh Policy for Promotion of Foreign Higher Educational Institutions and Private Universities,2026. Original policy text and individual UGC approvals were not obtained.','The policy seeks foreign and leading Indian private-university campuses,academic partnerships,research and investment.|The TOI report describes options involving government-selected academic infrastructure service providers.|Access to global-standard education within AP and avoidance of direct state financial burden are stated policy intentions,not independently established outcomes or proof that land/incentives have zero opportunity cost.','State facilitation,land provision,central higher-education regulation,qualification recognition and an individual campus approval are different legal/administrative layers.','Assess academic autonomy,quality,fees,student protection,research links and transparent incentives. A prestigious global brand does not automatically establish affordable access or strong local outcomes.|For APPSC,avoid treating targets/rankings from unverified coaching summaries as binding official criteria. Track published policy,UGC permissions,financial closure,faculty recruitment and actual student services before calling a campus operational.','Cabinet policy approval does not itself certify that a foreign university has opened or that all regulatory permissions exist.','What safeguards can make foreign-university facilitation improve equitable higher education?','APNIE,APTOI',7,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Higher education and student support','AP private-school reform: permission simplification does not repeal safety','The7OctoberTOIreport says Cabinet cleared promulgation of an ordinance amending the AP Education Act1982. The actual promulgated ordinance and commencement were not independently inspected.','The reported proposal removes preliminary survey/notification/need-establishment requirements for private-school permissions.|Safety,infrastructure,sanitation and prescribed educational standards are reported to continue.|Cabinet clearance,Governor promulgation,publication,commencement and later legislative replacement are distinct stages.','An ordinance under Article213is temporary legislation subject to constitutional conditions;Cabinet agreement alone is not the ordinance. Regulatory entry conditions differ from ongoing quality/safety duties.','Simpler entry may encourage supply,but credible inspection,disclosure,fees/grievance systems and child safety remain necessary. Administrative speed should not be measured only by approvals issued.|For APPSC,connect parental choice with equitable access and enforceable standards. Do not advise schools that every survey condition has legally disappeared without checking the operative instrument or claim that existing safety obligations are abolished.','The reviewed Cabinet decision is not independently verified promulgation;permission simplification is not blanket deregulation.','How should private-school entry reform preserve quality and child safety?','APTOI',7,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Higher education and student support','Thalliki Vandanam expansion: new inclusions and second-phase payments differ','TNIE’s8Octoberreport on the7OctoberCabinet gives revised2026–27coverage and administrative sanction. Original orders and individual bank receipts were not inspected.','The report adds58,601eligible students,taking coverage from67,47,190to68,05,791.|Administrative sanction rises from₹10,120.78crore to₹10,208.69crore,an increase of₹87.91crore.|The3,29,201second-phase beneficiaries mentioned for assistance are a separate group/count from the58,601new inclusions.','Beneficiary eligibility,administrative sanction,treasury release,successful bank credit and final receipt have different meanings. Student counts and mother/guardian payee counts should not be silently equated.','Audit exclusions,duplicates,school records and payment failures with accessible correction and appeal. A coverage increase is not proof that every eligible family is already included or that every sanctioned rupee has been paid.|For APPSC,retain the exact denominators and connect education support with attendance,learning and vulnerable households. This October expansion follows earlier scheme volumes;it is not a fresh first-ever launch of the programme.','58,601new inclusions do not equal all3,29,201second-phase beneficiaries;sanction is not audited disbursement.','How can education transfers combine reliable inclusion and meaningful learning outcomes?','APNIE,APTOI',7,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Higher education and student support','Welfare-hostel diet charges: a rate increase needs nutrition monitoring','The7OctoberCabinet reporting ratifies a10%increase in diet charges for hostels,residential schools and other welfare institutions. TOI reports effect from1October.','TNIE gives6,28,409students across relevant welfare departments as the reported coverage.|The10%increase concerns specified diet charges,not every education scholarship or a universal household food allowance.|Ratification,effective date,budget provision,supplier payment and actual meal quality require separate checks;original rate schedules were not read.','Nutrition support depends on menu quality,food safety,regular supply and age-specific needs,not only nominal expenditure per student. Food inflation can erode purchasing power.','Monitor protein/diversity,storage,hygiene,attendance-linked procurement and student feedback. Transparent inspections and grievance mechanisms can detect diversion and deteriorating meals.|For APPSC,connect the decision with tribal,BC,SC and other welfare systems without inventing identical rates for every institution. A10%budget-rate change does not prove a10%improvement in nutritional status.','The increase is not a universal10%rise in every student benefit or proof of completed supplier/beneficiary payments.','How should residential-student nutrition be assessed beyond diet-charge allocations?','APNIE,APTOI',7,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','01 · Higher education and student support','Marikavalasa tribal coaching: access must extend beyond examination seats','The7OctoberCabinet approves a Centre of Excellence at the AP Tribal Welfare Residential School in Marikavalasa,Visakhapatnam,according to the reviewed reports.','The proposed residential programme coaches600tribal Intermediate students for IIT-JEE and NEET.|TNIE reports equal seats for boys and girls,implying300each if the approved600-seat structure is implemented.|Cabinet approval does not establish completed buildings,filled seats,appointed faculty or verified examination results;original admission rules were not read.','Targeted coaching,school quality,language support,residential safety and admissions counselling address different barriers. A competitive-exam coaching centre is not a medical/engineering university.','Transparent selection,bridging support,qualified teaching,mental-health care and safe hostels matter alongside test preparation. Monitor retention and student wellbeing,not only topper publicity.|For APPSC,connect tribal education with foundational learning and equitable opportunity across remote habitations. Gender parity in seats should be assessed together with actual enrolment,safety and progression.','600approved seats are not600verified admissions to IITs/medical colleges or evidence the centre is already fully operational.','How can targeted residential coaching widen tribal students’ opportunities without neglecting foundational education?','APNIE,APTOI',7,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','02 · Horticulture data and public health systems','APHIMS: a horticulture platform needs usable and correct farmer data','The7OctoberCabinet approves the Andhra Pradesh Horticulture Information and Management System. Hans reports₹20.62crore over72months;TOI identifies design-to-maintenance responsibilities.','The system is intended to integrate collection,management and analysis of horticulture information and improve farmer services.|A72-month implementation/operations horizon is not proof that every service is already live.|The original contract,data architecture,procurement terms and farmer-level outcomes were not independently inspected.','A database,a decision-support model,farmer eligibility determination and a completed service transaction are distinct. Horticulture includes varied crops and seasonal production risks.','Use data minimisation,clear ownership,validation,assisted correction and interoperable standards. Avoid making a wrong parcel/crop entry an unchallengeable basis for denying a service.|For APPSC,link crop advisories,market planning,pest management and water use to field capacity. Measure accurate and timely services rather than dashboards or raw registrations;retain non-digital support for excluded cultivators.','₹20.62crore is a reported project outlay,not verified expenditure or proof of universal digital coverage.','What governance safeguards make agricultural information platforms useful and inclusive?','APHANS,APTOI',7,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','02 · Horticulture data and public health systems','ASHA gratuity: retain the death-in-service condition and cap','Hans’s7OctoberCabinet report explicitly describes gratuity for eligible ASHA workers who die while in service. The shorter TOI summary alone could be read too broadly.','The reported calculation is₹5,000for each completed year of service,subject to₹1.50lakh maximum.|The decision concerns the specified eligible death-in-service circumstances,not a verified universal retirement payout for every ASHA.|Original eligibility,nominee,documentation and payment orders were not read;actual disbursement is not established.','A capped gratuity,monthly honorarium,insurance benefit and pension are different forms of support. ASHA’s community-health role should not be equated automatically with permanent civil-service status.','Accessible claims,clear service records,nominee guidance and timely decisions matter to bereaved families. Wider workforce policy also concerns regular pay,safety,skills and grievance channels.|For APPSC,calculate the cap correctly:30completed years at₹5,000would reach₹1.50lakh,but this arithmetic does not determine actual eligibility. Do not promise a payment without checking the operative order.','The reviewed report specifies death in service;the cap is₹1.50lakh,not₹5,000permonth or an unlimited benefit.','How should community-health-worker social protection combine fair support and reliable delivery?','APHANS,APTOI',7,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','02 · Horticulture data and public health systems','Repalle hospital upgrade: beds and posts are inputs,not completed care','The7OctoberCabinet approves upgrading Repalle’s30-bed CHC to a100-bed Area Hospital. Hans reports₹58.42crore and66additional posts.','The planned bed increase is70,not100additional beds above the existing30.|Approved posts must be distinguished from recruited,trained and actually deployed staff.|Construction,equipment,diagnostics,referral links,blood availability and continuing operating funds determine service readiness;the original sanction was not inspected.','Community Health Centres and Area Hospitals occupy different service roles. Physical capacity differs from functional beds,occupancy,quality and patient outcomes.','Coordinate staffing,procurement,maintenance and referral transport rather than opening a building without dependable services. Accessible information should explain which services are genuinely available.|For APPSC,connect the upgrade to local need and district health planning without inventing completed commissioning or specialist availability. Monitor waiting times,avoidable referrals,infection prevention and affordability alongside bed counts.','30to100means70additional planned beds;66approved posts are not66verified appointments.','How should hospital upgrades be planned and evaluated as complete service systems?','APHANS,APTOI',7,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','03 · Land administration and public assets','NTR poramboke regularisation: eligibility does not erase public-purpose safeguards','The7OctoberCabinet ratifies orders for specified unobjectionable government poramboke occupations in NTR district,according to the reviewed reports. Original orders and exclusion schedules were not inspected.','The report describes a one-time regularisation/reclassification decision for the specified scope,not every occupation of public land.|“Unobjectionable”must be read against the actual legal eligibility and protected-land exclusions,not assumed from private possession.|Ratification,individual eligibility verification,allotment,title documents and possession are separate stages.','Government land classification,common/public-purpose use and private rights have different legal implications. Long occupation alone does not establish an unconditional right to regularisation.','Publish boundaries,eligibility,cut-offs and grievance procedures while protecting drains,water bodies,roads and other public functions. Fair process should address both vulnerable occupants and competing public claims.|For APPSC,avoid treating a district-specific reported decision as blanket statewide amnesty. No exact payment rate,cut-off date or universal exemption is certified without the original instrument.','Regularising specified unobjectionable occupations is not permission to privatise every water body or government parcel.','How can land regularisation protect vulnerable residents while preserving public assets?','APTOI,APHANS',7,True,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','03 · Land administration and public assets','Cabinet land allocations: match extent,purpose and district accurately','The7Octoberreports list distinct land decisions for Kuppam,Kadapa and Prakasam. These are not one combined interchangeable land bank.','Kuppam:reported1.69acres at Nakkanapalle for a Kuppam Area Development Authority officials’guest house.|YSR Kadapa:reported7.66acres at Pathur/Pathuru for Endowments Department temple development.|Prakasam:reported7acres at Kondapi/Kondepi for a boys’residential school/hostel. Original surveys,conditions and transfer records were not inspected.','Reclassification,departmental transfer,allotment and construction are separate administrative actions. Alternative spellings in reporting should not be turned into invented new locations.','Review opportunity cost,suitability,public purpose,access and environmental risk before counting an allocation as service delivery. Survey verification and published conditions can reduce later disputes.|For APPSC,retain district–purpose–extent pairs rather than memorising one total stripped of context. Cabinet approval does not establish a completed school,functioning guest house or final legally uncontested title.','7.66acres for temple development is not the7-acre school/hostel decision;they concern different districts and purposes.','What transparency standards should govern public-land allocations?','APTOI,APHANS',7,True,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','04 · Cyclones,restoration and resilient coasts','Cyclone Arnab: separate an evolving forecast from confirmed landfall','IMD’s early28Octoberbulletin forecasts crossing near the AP–Yanam coast;the Indian Express later reports completed landfall on28Octoberand subsequent weakening.','The inspected IMD bulletin was issued0130ISTon28Octoberusing2330IST27Octoberobservations. Its narrative forecasts125–135km/h sustained wind,gusting150,near landfall.|The forecast corridor is between Bapatla and Visakhapatnam,close to south of Yanam;the exact final track and observed maximum require later official records.|The later newspaper report confirms landfall completion and weakening. Forecast tables contain some wind/category inconsistencies,so they are not reused as a definitive observed classification record.','Landfall is a process involving the cyclone centre crossing the coast;rain,storm surge,wind and inland flooding have different footprints and timelines. Yanam belongs to Puducherry,not AP.','Warnings must be timestamped and updated. Evacuation,shelters,health support,fisher advisories and resilient power/communications should follow local exposure,not only the centre’s predicted line.|For APPSC,study the event without treating early rainfall/casualty totals as final audited losses. Later November CycloneMurasucompletion should not be silently inserted as an October event.','A forecast speed/location is not a verified final observation;weakening after landfall does not end flood or electrical risks.','How should cyclone communication translate uncertain forecasts into timely protective action?','IMD,ARNAB',28,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','04 · Cyclones,restoration and resilient coasts','Arnab power restoration: mutual assistance needs safe energisation','The inspected Indian Express liveblog passage reports Telangana’s28Octoberassurance of personnel/material support for AP restoration after Arnab. It is reported assistance,not an audited inventory of deployment.','Damage to poles,trees,roads and networks creates interacting access and safety problems.|The report describes a flash-flood-risk warning continuing after landfall;weather and water conditions can affect repair crews and public access.|Promises of support,actual dispatch,completed repair,inspection and safe supply restoration must be distinguished.','Network resilience includes redundancy,spares,crew capability,communication and safe operating procedures. Power restoration and indiscriminate re-energisation are not the same.','Prioritise hospitals,water systems,shelters and communications while isolating unsafe equipment. Coordinate states,DISCOMs,local authorities and emergency responders with clear responsibility and documented handovers.|For APPSC,connect response with longer-term flood/cyclone-informed network design. Do not claim every household had power restored merely because a support announcement was issued or a feeder was energised.','A cross-state assistance assurance is not proof of completed restoration or a final damage estimate.','What makes post-cyclone lifeline restoration both rapid and safe?','ARNAB',28,True)
add('Andhra Pradesh','04 · Cyclones,restoration and resilient coasts','AP application: align coastal insurance with warnings and real loss exposure','This is an original AP application of the October risk-finance discussion and Arnab reporting,not an independently verified new state insurance scheme.','Parametric triggers could provide quick liquidity,but coastal households and fishers can face losses outside a narrowly defined wind/rain threshold.|Crop,livestock,housing,fishing assets and business interruption have different coverage needs.|Relief funds,insurance,premium subsidies and loans are distinct tools;the reviewed sources do not establish universal enrolment or new guaranteed state payouts.','Basis risk,affordability,insured value,exclusions and claims access determine financial protection. Insurance reduces financial exposure,not the physical cyclone hazard.','Use trustworthy local observations,plain-language contracts and inclusive distribution. Combine finance with shelters,resilient assets,coastal planning and transparent damage assessment.|For APPSC,compare quick parametric payment against loss-based assessment rather than assuming one mechanism is always superior. Track whether vulnerable groups can claim and rebuild without shifting unmanaged public liabilities to future budgets.','This is policy analysis,not an October AP Cabinet announcement or evidence that all cyclone losses are insured.','Design a layered financial-protection strategy for Andhra Pradesh’s coastal communities.','MAG,IMD,ARNAB',ap=True,p='P2')
add('Andhra Pradesh','05 · State energy and consumer safeguards','AP application: hydrogen pilots need network evidence and fair billing','This is original AP policy application of PNGRB’s October draft,not a confirmed state rollout or permission for household experimentation.','A5%by-volume commercial ceiling in a draft is not automatic authority for every local gas network.|The responsible entity must establish network,equipment,meter and appliance suitability under the applicable statutory/approval process.|Transparent calorific-value treatment,advance information,monitoring and safe isolation matter to household service quality.','A geographic gas authorisation,a hydrogen pilot,a commercial approval and actual supply are separate stages. Volume-based gas delivery and useful heat are not identical.','Start with bounded,independently reviewed trials and clear stop/restart criteria. Assess hydrogen production emissions,safety,consumer complaints and alternatives before claiming decarbonisation success.|For APPSC,connect pipeline-safety lessons,DISCOM integration and consumer protection without claiming a new Guntur,Visakhapatnamorother city project that was not verified. Public communication must make the draft status and limits clear.','A national draft does not prove hydrogen-blended gas is already supplied statewide or that every appliance is compatible.','How should Andhra Pradesh evaluate safe and accountable hydrogen-blending opportunities?','H2,PRS',ap=True,p='P2')

